gned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+4\\cdot f(4)+6\\cdot f(6)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{10})+4(\\frac{1}{5})+6(\\frac{3}{10})+8(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{8}{10}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+18+32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{10} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382289 13194139536072 true] [17592186382288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536072 true] [17592186382288 1041 "field-1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382289 13194139536072 true] [17592186382288 1110 #uuid "63213b56-9578-55ee-83ef-fbb4fd4a7d67" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382289 1034 "6" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382289 1111 #uuid "616b5048-9295-5d55-b837-59f54658254a" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382291 13194139536072 true] [17592186382290 1089 "q-337208" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536072 true] [17592186382290 1100 "A jar has $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve. If one chip is randomly drawn from the jar,  what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382290 1113 #uuid "fae6621b-3d62-575e-8e5f-44d32abfbcdc" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382290 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn chip. Then the support is $S={1,4,9,12}.$\n\nIn the jar, there are $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve (fifteen chips in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =\\frac{6}{15}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(9) & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(12) & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(4)+9\\cdot f(9)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+4(\\frac{4}{15})+9(\\frac{1}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{27}{15}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+16+27+24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382292 13194139536072 true] [17592186382291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536072 true] [17592186382291 1041 "field-1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382292 13194139536072 true] [17592186382291 1110 #uuid "6c488644-45f2-51b8-b365-61fc8200c8e9" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382292 1034 "\\frac{73}{15}" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382292 1111 #uuid "b3562320-3ec1-5f3f-9de2-c41f240a2e6d" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382293 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382294 13194139536072 true] [17592186382293 1089 "q-337334" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382293 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536072 true] [17592186382293 1100 "Consider the following game featuring an unfair six-sided die. The probability that the die shows an odd number is $\\frac{2}{3},$ and the probability that the die shows an even number is $\\frac{1}{3}.$ If the roll of a die gives an odd number, the player loses $$4.$ If the roll of a die gives an even number, the player wins $$11.$ What is the expected profit of each roll in this game?\n\n$\\text{E}[p]=$${{field-1}}" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382293 1113 #uuid "1164e969-cccf-5630-b221-713e2082da7f" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382293 1145 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$, $4,$ or $6$), the player wins $$11,$ so we have $p=11.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-4)=\\frac{2}{3},\\,P(11)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-4$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-4)\\cdot f(-4)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =(-4)(\\frac{2}{3})+11(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$1.00.$" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382294 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382295 13194139536072 true] [17592186382294 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536072 true] [17592186382294 1041 "field-1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382294 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382295 13194139536072 true] [17592186382294 1110 #uuid "951b612e-0bfa-5844-96ef-8ffb914f2bd1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382295 1034 "1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382295 1111 #uuid "8bc54023-34b8-5f61-a3cc-c8e79f6838fe" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382296 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382297 13194139536072 true] [17592186382296 1089 "q-337335" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382296 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536072 true] [17592186382296 1100 "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2.$ The outcomes of the coin have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{1}{4},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382296 1113 #uuid "96186115-d57c-502e-bc38-875002051c61" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382296 1145 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8,$ so we have $p=8.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $8$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =8\\cdot f(8)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+(-2)(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4}-\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$0.50.$" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382298 13194139536072 true] [17592186382297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382299 13194139536072 true] [17592186382297 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536072 true] [17592186382297 1041 "field-1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382297 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382299 13194139536072 true] [17592186382297 1110 #uuid "f63db224-f262-5a03-95ab-e907ec7cb4a1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382298 1034 "3" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382298 1111 #uuid "cbdca36d-00a9-5e02-b6e1-69c0c2e9327e" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382299 1034 "0.5" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382299 1111 #uuid "1a3294e6-a9ad-5444-ae01-a78526641b6a" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382300 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382301 13194139536072 true] [17592186382300 1089 "q-337336" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382300 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536072 true] [17592186382300 1100 "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4.$ The coin outcomes have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382300 1113 #uuid "46a9b718-b51f-5916-aeef-799dfe44f90d" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382300 1145 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{7}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $5$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =5\\cdot f(5)+(-4)\\cdot f(-4) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{10})+(-4)(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{28}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-28}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =-1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.30.$" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382302 13194139536072 true] [17592186382301 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536072 true] [17592186382301 1041 "field-1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382302 13194139536072 true] [17592186382301 1110 #uuid "f04e6493-8275-577c-bc4a-71282a0bf962" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382302 1034 "1.3" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382302 1111 #uuid "a5149b29-7520-5e09-890a-e16a02c0d031" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382303 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382304 13194139536072 true] [17592186382303 1089 "q-337338" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382303 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536072 true] [17592186382303 1100 "Suppose we play a game that costs $$2$ per round. Each player randomly selects one card from a standard $52$-card deck. If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. What is the expected profit of a player in this game?\n\nRounded to two decimal places, the expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382303 1113 #uuid "ec94eab8-de30-55c3-9f26-b985845c0e04" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382303 1145 "Let $p$ represent the profit in this game. There are $52$ possible cards that can be selected. One of these cards is the winning card. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ This means the player’s profit is $$100-$2=$98,$ so we have $p=98.$\n\n- If the ace of spades is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(98)=\\frac{1}{52},\\,P(-2)=\\frac{51}{52}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $98$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{52}$ | $\\frac{51}{52}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =98\\cdot f(98)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =98(\\frac{1}{52})-2(\\frac{51}{52}) \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{52}-\\frac{102}{52} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is approximately $-$\\,0.08.$" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382305 13194139536072 true] [17592186382304 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536072 true] [17592186382304 1041 "field-1" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382304 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382305 13194139536072 true] [17592186382304 1110 #uuid "ae04186d-50e4-5f9a-ae88-fdcf78897eb4" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382305 1034 "0.08" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536072 true] [17592186382305 1111 #uuid "a7c4dfa8-de50-5d99-86df-f131eeea8d9f" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382306 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382307 13194139536072 true] [17592186382306 1089 "q-83641" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382306 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536072 true] [17592186382306 1100 "Several egg cartons were checked for cracked eggs. The number of cracked eggs in each carton was recorded below. If $N$ is the number of cracked eggs in a carton, what is the expected value of $N?$\n\n| Number of Cartons | $10$ | $7$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cracked Eggs | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382306 1113 #uuid "b623616a-4d3b-5c1a-8acf-5c478617b975" 13194139536072 true] [17592186382306 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the cracked eggs, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of cartons is $10+7+2+1=20,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(N=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(N=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{7}{20} \\\\ P(N=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{2}{20}=\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(N=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the product